00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:03.810
When working with Word documents, there
may be occasions where you need to

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:06.420
insert information in a table format.

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:10.380
There are two ways of doing
this within Word, and the first

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:11.520
way is quite straightforward.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:17.970
So if I go up to the insert tab and I
selected insert, and then on the table

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:24.285
icon, selected insert table, I can
then create a table with the number and

00:00:24.285 --> 00:00:26.595
columns and rows that I would require.

00:00:27.075 --> 00:00:30.465
So as long as I knew how many columns
and rows I needed, I could insert that.

00:00:31.725 --> 00:00:33.165
I just select that as an example.

00:00:33.495 --> 00:00:37.785
And as you can see, it is inserted
five columns and six rows.

00:00:38.825 --> 00:00:42.245
The problem there is I still need to
populate the table with the information

00:00:42.665 --> 00:00:46.555
so if I just undo that by Command and Z.

00:00:47.025 --> 00:00:50.235
An alternative to that is to
actually import the information

00:00:50.415 --> 00:00:52.125
and format it as a table.

00:00:52.935 --> 00:00:55.965
So I have the information
that I wish to enter into the

00:00:55.965 --> 00:00:57.255
letter within this spreadsheet.

00:00:57.825 --> 00:01:02.955
So I'm just going to highlight the
information and use Command and C to copy.

00:01:04.095 --> 00:01:07.875
And then once I've copied that
I can go back to the letter and

00:01:07.935 --> 00:01:12.165
place the cursor where I want the
information to be entered, and

00:01:12.165 --> 00:01:14.535
then I will use the Command and V.

00:01:15.315 --> 00:01:18.675
So as you can see that has now pasted
the the information into the letter,

00:01:18.735 --> 00:01:22.125
but it's pretty much in the same format
as it was within the spreadsheet.

00:01:22.145 --> 00:01:23.625
So it doesn't look very presentable.

00:01:24.465 --> 00:01:27.225
There are a number of paste
options, and you'll see that by

00:01:27.225 --> 00:01:28.545
the little clipboard at the bottom.

00:01:28.605 --> 00:01:32.655
So if I click on that, I can keep the
source formatting which is what it's

00:01:32.655 --> 00:01:38.355
doing at the moment, match destination
table style, paste as a picture, keep

00:01:38.355 --> 00:01:42.825
the text only, keep source formatting
and link it to Excel, so it would look

00:01:42.825 --> 00:01:46.275
the same, but it would be linked to
the spreadsheet, match destination

00:01:46.275 --> 00:01:48.105
table, style and link to Excel.

00:01:48.105 --> 00:01:51.525
So that would put it into a
table within the document and

00:01:51.525 --> 00:01:52.845
then still link it to Excel.

00:01:52.845 --> 00:01:56.055
So that meant that if any of
the data changed within the

00:01:56.505 --> 00:01:59.505
spreadsheet, it would then update
in the table within the letter.

00:02:00.205 --> 00:02:03.475
So for this example, I'm just going to
keep the text only so I can demonstrate

00:02:03.475 --> 00:02:06.025
how you can convert text to a table.

00:02:06.595 --> 00:02:09.865
So I'll keep the text and as you can
see, it doesn't look very presentable

00:02:09.865 --> 00:02:11.215
and it's not very well laid out.

00:02:12.025 --> 00:02:17.215
So what I need to do is I now need to
highlight all of that information and

00:02:17.215 --> 00:02:25.195
then go up to the insert tab and select
table, and then convert text to table.

00:02:25.975 --> 00:02:29.970
So if I click on that now, it will ask
me how many columns and rows I want.

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:34.380
So it actually knows there are three
columns and eight rows required

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:35.850
because it's already worked it out.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:41.280
And the column width is
auto and it is separated.

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:42.640
Text at tabs.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.740
That basically means that in between
each of these instances, there is

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:51.180
a tab symbol and just to show that
if I click cancel, and I go to the

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:57.959
home menu and then select the show
hide button from the paragraph tools.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:03.359
As you can see, there's a little
tab icon in between each of those.

00:03:03.434 --> 00:03:10.064
That is what's allowing us to identify
that it is three columns and eight rows.

00:03:10.244 --> 00:03:17.624
So if I just take that back off there,
go back to the insert table, convert

00:03:17.624 --> 00:03:23.694
text to table, three columns, eight
rows, separated by tabs, click OK.

00:03:24.734 --> 00:03:27.644
And that has created a table
with the information included.

00:03:28.274 --> 00:03:35.084
So from there, when I'm on the
actual table design tab, I can then

00:03:35.084 --> 00:03:39.704
change the style so I can apply
different table styles to the table.

00:03:41.134 --> 00:03:44.804
I can expand that so I can have
a wider range of table styles.

00:03:45.194 --> 00:03:47.414
So I'll just for example, select that one.

00:03:48.614 --> 00:03:51.354
And again, if I want to make any more
changes, just double click on the

00:03:51.354 --> 00:03:57.534
table, you could add shading, border
styles, different pen colors, and

00:03:57.714 --> 00:04:01.304
line widths, and various other things
that you may wish to experiment with.

00:04:01.754 --> 00:04:06.434
But that is how you can insert a
table from Excel into a document and

00:04:06.434 --> 00:04:08.384
format it to make it look presentable.

